<h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold text-left font-display text-blue-750 sm:text-3xl text-balance">2. Assessment as learning: Active tasks</h2>
<p>
Combining learning tasks with assessment tasks ensures that students will focus on the learning. 

<strong>Why?</strong>
Because students are efficient. Assessment tasks are the focus of student effort and non-assessed learning tasks are generally ignored. 

<img src="/public/MATTERImages/examplesOfCombinedTasks.png" />

Learning resources/materials/opportunities are provided in whatever manner you prefer (textbooks, websites, video lessons, in person lectures, workshop activities, etc.) for students to refer to when attempting the tasks. MATTER does not prescribe how you must present your content.
</p>

<h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold text-left font-display text-blue-750 sm:text-3xl text-balance">3. Trusted evidence: process, discussion, and observation</h2>
<p>The iterative formative nature of the OnTrack Framework enables unit teaching staff to interact with and observe individual students’ growth over time. In this framework each student has, essentially, a personal learning coach - a staff member who is assigned to provide feedback and advice on their work over the course of the unit. They interact with the student in person in classes, and are responsible for providing advice to that student on the quality of their work both online and in person. This makes the student’s learning process visible which can then be verified via observation and discussion of the work. Because tasks are designed to capture the full range of skills and knowledge required in the unit, staff can verify that the student has achieved mastery of the unit learning requirements by being able to knowledgeably discuss their work on each task.</p>
  <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold text-left font-display text-blue-750 sm:text-3xl text-balance">4. Reflexivity: freedom to fail, build feedback literacy, iterative feedback</h2>
<p>
Building in iterative formative feedback loops ensures that students can experiment, practice and improve their skills under guidance in a supportive low stakes environment
<strong>Why?</strong>
Because failing is learning. Teach students to not to fear mistakes, and to value advice. Enable them to build mastery by receiving and acting upon rich iterative feedback from an expert (you), and correcting their mistakes.
<p>Examples that illustrate the point:</p>
</p>
<p><img src="/public/MATTERImages/ExampleIterativeFeedbackDepth.png" /></p>

<p><img src="/public/MATTERImages/ExampleIterativeFeedbackBreadth.png" /></p>

<h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold text-left font-display text-blue-750 sm:text-3xl text-balance">5. Holistic perspective: capture student's growth, incorporate personas, treat students as whole people, student agency</h2>
<p>The OnTrack Education Framework enables a holistic approach to learning. It treats students as individuals with agency and responsibility for their own learning and enables a focus on student growth as a whole person. This is the fundamental underpinning that enables students to achieve far more than they might be able to in a traditional unit structure. 
Student achievements are defined by personas that represent what the student will know, think and be able to do at each achievement level. This is described holistically within the context of the unit content and discipline. A passing level student, regardless of the discipline is described as fundamentally competent with the requirements of the unit. Students achieving at higher levels may be described as having a deeper or broader command of the concepts, or of being able to synthesise, extrapolate or create new knowledge. 
Example: [show persona statements]
</p>

